      "S3",
      "S5"
    ]
  },
  "long_biography": {
    "plain_text": "Money-transfer entrepreneur Paolo Orlandi initially wanted to open a restaurant in California. When the cost put that plan beyond reach, he obtained a currency-exchange licence instead. Working with his wife, Cindy, he developed the operation from a small Los Angeles office into a network reaching customers in Mexico.\n\nHis approach to reaching customers included football sponsorship. Orlandi Valuta became a corporate partner of Major League Soccer, securing pitch-side signage and advertising in match programmes. Orlandi explained that football's popularity among Mexican customers made the arrangement commercially useful. The company also made individual agreements with teams in Los Angeles, Dallas, San Jose and Tampa Bay.",
    "html": "<p>Money-transfer entrepreneur Paolo Orlandi initially wanted to open a restaurant in California. When the cost put that plan beyond reach, he obtained a currency-exchange licence instead. Working with his wife, Cindy, he developed the operation from a small Los Angeles office into a network reaching customers in Mexico.</p>\r\n<p>His approach to reaching customers included football sponsorship. Orlandi Valuta became a corporate partner of Major League Soccer, securing pitch-side signage and advertising in match programmes. Orlandi explained that football's popularity among Mexican customers made the arrangement commercially useful. The company also made individual agreements with teams in Los Angeles, Dallas, San Jose and Tampa Bay.</p>\r\n",
